    "available_at": "2026-10-19T16:48:02.097568+00:00",
    "body": "Ares Management has agreed to buy Infinity Transportation, a railcar and intermodal equipment leasing company, from KKR for about $2.9 billion, the companies announced Monday.\nWhat’s Related\nInfinity owns and manages about 50,000 railcars. It leases them to more than 350 customers in the U.S., Canada and Mexico .\nThe deal is expected to close in the first quarter of 2027, pending approvals and other standard conditions. Ares Infrastructure Equity and Ares Insurance Solutions will manage the funds for the purchase.\n“Infinity’s railcar portfolio is a critical component of the U.S. transportation and logistics supply chain,” said Mike Roth, partner in Ares Infrastructure Equity, and Anup Agarwal, head and chief investment officer of Ares Insurance Solutions.\nWhy Ares did the deal\nAres plans to fund growth in Infinity’s fleet and pursue more acquisitions once the deal closes. Roth and Agarwal pointed to reshoring , new domestic industrial investment, and the push for sturdier supply chains as reasons they expect railcar demand to hold up over the long run. They also said the deal builds on Ares’ growing activity in the rail sector.\nInfinity was founded in 2004 and is based in Atlanta. KKR, through its Global Atlantic subsidiary, has been invested in the company since 2016. Starting in 2021, KKR helped Infinity build out its own standalone operation, grow its fleet and raise its value by 150%.\n“We appreciate KKR’s partnership and support, which enabled us to invest in the team, expand our fleet and build Infinity into the industry leader it is today,” said Jeff Edelman, president and CEO of Infinity. Edelman added that he’s looking forward to starting a new chapter with Ares.\nMacquarie Capital and Simpson Thacher & Bartlett advised KKR on the sale. Citi and Kirkland & Ellis advised Ares.",
